From 529a176d1e72433b2dd7522d38ac794f01f2cb22 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: a70q <noreply@example.org>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 22:36:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] loop: rescan partitions after LOOP_SET_BLOCK_SIZE

On 4.14 there is no LOOP_CONFIGURE, so util-linux losetup -P --sector-size
falls back to LOOP_SET_FD + LOOP_SET_STATUS64 (LO_FLAGS_PARTSCAN, partitions
scanned with 512-byte sectors) and only then LOOP_SET_BLOCK_SIZE. The block
size change never triggered a rescan, so a GPT written with 4096-byte sectors
(pmOS subpartitions on a 4K-native UFS userdata) showed no partitions.
---
 drivers/block/loop.c | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/block/loop.c b/drivers/block/loop.c
index 6409c96a7..5a557be10 100755
--- a/drivers/block/loop.c
+++ b/drivers/block/loop.c
@@ -1472,6 +1472,14 @@ static int loop_set_block_size(struct loop_device *lo, unsigned long arg)
 out_unfreeze:
 	blk_mq_unfreeze_queue(lo->lo_queue);
 
+	/*
+	 * Partitions were scanned with the old logical block size when
+	 * LO_FLAGS_PARTSCAN was set (LOOP_SET_FD/LOOP_SET_STATUS64 come first
+	 * on kernels without LOOP_CONFIGURE), so rescan with the new one.
+	 */
+	if (!err && (lo->lo_flags & LO_FLAGS_PARTSCAN))
+		loop_reread_partitions(lo, lo->lo_device);
+
 	return err;
 }
 
